use std
into surfacing.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with su_ is exported). It holds the geometry decisions for surfacing features: for knitting loose faces into a solid it measures the gap at every free (unshared) edge and picks the sewing tolerance, and for wrapping a flat profile onto a flat or cylindrical face it works out where the unrolled profile goes and whether it fits. Its only import line is `import math;`, ending with a semicolon. Every int used in real math is converted with `as real`, and a real used as an int with `as int`, since Helium has no real or int conversion calls.

```amni
extern fn su_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "su_view_f64";
extern fn su_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "su_view_i32";
```

Module state. Ints, all starting at 0: `NE` (free edges) and `NK` (samples per edge, at least 2). Growable f64 arrays: `IN` (32 inputs), `OUT` (16 outputs), `EP` (3 NK per edge: points sampled along free edge i, sample m at 3 times (i times NK plus m)), `BB` (6 per edge: the smallest x, y and z and the largest x, y and z of its samples) and `GP` (NE gaps). Growable i32 array `PT` (NE: for each edge, the edge nearest its middle sample, or -1).

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x. `zi(xs, n, x)` does the same for an i32 growable array, resizing to n entries equal to x as i32. On the web an array that holds entries cannot grow past the room it first reserved, so every array is emptied before it is sized again. `at1(n)` returns n, or 1 when n is below 1.

The exported function `su_views()`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 result, registers the arrays with `su_view_f64`: 1 IN, 2 OUT, 3 EP, 4 GP; and with `su_view_i32`: 20 PT. It returns 0.

The exported function `su_init()`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 result, sizes IN to 32 zeros and OUT to 16 zeros with `zf`, calls `su_views()` and returns 0.

The exported function `su_size(ne, nk)`, declared with the export keyword with two i32 parameters (each converted with `as int` at the top) and an i32 result, sets NE to ne and NK to nk raised to at least 2, then sizes, each size raised to at least 1 with `at1`: with `zf` EP to 3 times ne times NK, BB to 6 ne and GP to ne, all zeros; with `zi` PT to ne filled with -1. It calls `su_views()` and returns 0.

Distance helper. `segd(px, py, pz, j, m)` returns the distance from the point to the segment from sample m to sample m plus 1 of edge j: with the segment vector d and the point less the segment start v, the fraction w is the dot product of v and d over the length of d squared, clamped to 0.0 to 1.0 (0.0 when the length squared is not above 1e-30), and the result is the distance from the point to the segment start plus w times d. `boxd(px, py, pz, j)` returns the distance from the point to edge j's box BB (0.0 inside it): the length of the per-axis amounts by which the point lies below the smallest or above the largest value.

The exported function `su_gaps()`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 result, measures the gap at each free edge. Input IN[0] is the largest gap that still counts as a seam to close (the cap). First BB is filled for each edge from its samples. Then for each edge i, its gap GP[i] is the largest, over its samples m, of the smallest distance from sample m to any other edge j (j not i), measured with segd over every segment of edge j; an edge j is skipped for a sample when boxd from the sample to edge j is not below the best distance found so far for that sample. The best distance starts at 1e300; when there is no other edge the gap is 1e300. PT[i] becomes the edge j nearest to edge i's middle sample (sample NK over 2, as int division), or -1 when there is none. Then OUT[2] is the largest GP that is not above the cap (0.0 when there is none), OUT[1] counts the edges whose GP is above the cap, OUT[3] is the smallest GP above the cap (0.0 when there is none) and OUT[0], the sewing tolerance, is 1.1 times OUT[2] plus 1e-9 times (1.0 plus the cap). Returns OUT[1] as int.

The exported function `su_place()`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 result, places a flat profile on a face in its unrolled coordinates, where x runs along the face (for a cylinder, arc length around it, which the face maps to its angle u times its radius) and y along the face's second direction (for a cylinder, height along its axis). Inputs: IN[0] the face kind (1 flat, 2 cylinder), IN[1] the cylinder radius r, IN[2] and IN[3] the anchor's face parameters u0 and v0, IN[4], IN[5], IN[6] and IN[7] the profile's box (smallest x, smallest y, largest x, largest y) after any rotation, IN[8] 1.0 when the cylinder's frame is right-handed, IN[9] the anchor mode (0 the box center goes to the anchor, 1 the profile's own origin goes to the anchor), IN[10], IN[11], IN[12] and IN[13] the face's parameter bounds (umin, umax, vmin, vmax).

The anchor in unrolled coordinates is (ax, ay): for a flat face (u0, v0); for a cylinder, ay is v0 and ax is r times u0 when IN[8] is above 0.5, and r times (2 pi minus u0) otherwise. The reference point (cx, cy) is the box center in mode 0 and (0.0, 0.0) in mode 1. OUT[0] becomes ax minus cx and OUT[1] ay minus cy (the shift to apply to the profile). The width w is IN[6] minus IN[4]. For a cylinder OUT[2] is w over r (the angle the profile wraps through, 0.0 when r is not above 1e-12), and 0.0 for a flat face. The flags start at 0: plus 1 when the face is a cylinder and w is above 2 pi times r times (1.0 minus 1e-9) (the profile is longer than the circumference); plus 2 when the profile's shifted y range (IN[5] plus OUT[1] to IN[7] plus OUT[1]) reaches past the face's v bounds by more than 1e-7 times (1.0 plus the absolute value of IN[13] minus IN[12]); plus 4 when the face is flat and the shifted x range (IN[4] plus OUT[0] to IN[6] plus OUT[0]) reaches past the face's u bounds by more than 1e-7 times (1.0 plus the absolute value of IN[11] minus IN[10]), or when the face is a cylinder whose u range (IN[11] minus IN[10]) is below 2 pi times (1.0 minus 1e-9) and the profile's angular range around the anchor (u0 less w over 2 r to u0 plus w over 2 r in mode 0, or u0 plus IN[4] over r to u0 plus IN[6] over r in mode 1, with each end taken relative to IN[10] and wrapped into 0.0 to 2 pi) does not stay inside the face's u range. OUT[3] becomes the flags as real. Returns the flags as int.

The `main()` function does nothing.
